      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find two integers whose least common multiple (LCM) is 36 and whose greatest common divisor (GCD) is 6. Let's denote these two integers by \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) = 36\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(a, b) = 6\n\\]\n\nRecall the relationship between the LCM and GCD of two integers:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) \\times \\text{GCD}(a, b) = a \\times b\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values into this relationship, we get:\n\\[\n36 \\times 6 = a \\times b\n\\]\n\nCalculating the left-hand side:\n\\[\n36 \\times 6 = 216\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\na \\times b = 216\n\\]\n\nTo find \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we need more information or constraints. However, since the problem does not provide additional constraints, we assume \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are coprime (i.e., \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) share no common factors other than 1), except for the factor of 6.\n\nGiven \\(\\text{GCD}(a, b) = 6\\), we express \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) in terms of their primes and multiples:\n\\[\na = 6m \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 6n\n\\]\nwhere \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are coprime integers.\n\nWe substitute \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) into the product equation:\n\\[\n(6m) \\times (6n) = 216\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n36mn = 216\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by 36:\n\\[\nmn = 6\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are coprime, the possible pairs \\((m, n)\\) that multiply to 6 are:\n\\[\n(m, n) = (1, 6), (2, 3), (3, 2), (6, 1)\n\\]\n\nThus, the pairs \\((a, b)\\) are:\n\\[\n(a, b) = (6 \\times 1, 6 \\times 6) = (6, 36)\n\\]\n\\[\n(a, b) = (6 \\times 2, 6 \\times 3) = (12, 18)\n\\]\n\\[\n(a, b) = (6 \\times 3, 6 \\times 2) = (18, 12)\n\\]\n\\[\n(a, b) = (6 \\times 6, 6 \\times 1) = (36, 6)\n\\]\n\nIn all cases, the product \\(a \\times b\\) remains:\n\\[\n216\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the two numbers is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{216}\n\\]",
